# Q3 – Effect of Training Dataset Size on Neural Network Performance

In this section, the effect of training dataset size on the performance of a
neural network classifier is investigated.

While neural networks are often described as data-hungry models, it is not
always clear how much training data is required before performance begins to
saturate. Understanding this relationship is particularly important in
astronomical surveys, where labelled data can be expensive to obtain.

This investigation focuses on how classification accuracy changes as the
number of training samples is increased.

## Research question

How does the size of the training dataset affect the classification accuracy
of a neural network trained to distinguish between stars, galaxies, and quasars?

import numpy as np
import torch
from torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader
import torch.nn as nn
import torch.optim as optim

from py.functions import load_star_data, prepare_features, split_data, scale_features, encode_labels

df = load_star_data()

drop_cols = ["obj_ID"]
X, y = prepare_features(df, target_col="class", drop_cols=drop_cols)

X_train, X_val, X_test, y_train, y_val, y_test = split_data(
    X, y, test_size=0.2, val_size=0.2, random_state=42
)

X_train_s, X_val_s, X_test_s, scaler = scale_features(X_train, X_val, X_test)

y_train_e, y_val_e, y_test_e, le = encode_labels(y_train, y_val, y_test)

## Experimental setup

The neural network architecture and training procedure used in Q2 are reused
here. Only the size of the training dataset is varied, while the validation and
test sets are kept fixed.

This ensures that any changes in performance can be attributed to the amount of
training data rather than differences in model architecture or evaluation.


